The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
handler = create_flow_rule(dev, ft_prio, &leftovers_specs[LEFTOVERS_MC].flow_attr, dst);
if (!IS_ERR(handler) && flow_attr->type == IB_FLOW_ATTR_ALL_DEFAULT) {
    handler_ucast = create_flow_rule(dev, ft_prio, &leftovers_specs[LEFTOVERS_UC].flow_attr, dst);
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because when the attempt to create a unicast flow rule (`handler_ucast`) fails, the code does not delete the previously created multicast flow rule (`handler`). This results in a resource leak since the multicast flow rule remains allocated without being freed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
mlx5_del_flow_rule(handler->rule);
kfree(handler);
```
Reason:
The fix involves explicitly deleting the multicast flow rule (`handler`) and freeing its associated memory when the creation of the unicast flow rule fails. This ensures that resources are properly released, preventing the resource leak that was occurring in the original code.

